	{
		id = "field_note_kestrel",
		title = "Field Notes, Kestrel Valley",
		kind = "journal",
		meta = "Dr. Marcus Hale, Regional Geological Survey. September 1979.",
		blocks = {
			{ heading = "Sept. 14" },
			"Arrived at the valley floor a little after noon. Locals call it Kestrel, though nobody could tell me why. There are no kestrels. There are no birds at all, which I noted only because the silence was so complete that I heard my own boots as if someone were walking just behind me.",
			"The impact site is smaller than the reports suggested. A shallow bowl perhaps forty metres across, the rim glassed in places, as if the ground had been briefly asked to be something else and had agreed. No crater debris. No scattering. Whatever fell here did not break anything. It *settled*.",
			{ heading = "Sept. 15" },
			"There is an object at the centre of the bowl, half-buried, and I have spent most of today not writing about it, because I don't know how.",
			"It is a crystal, or it behaves like one. Pale, long, faceted along lines that don't meet where they should. At first I thought it was reflecting the sky. Then the clouds came over and it went on reflecting a sky that wasn't there anymore. Blue and clear and slightly too bright.",
			"It is warm. Not hot. Warm the way a hand is warm.",
			{ figure = "valley_sketch", caption = "Sketch of the bowl, west rim. The object is marked at centre. (Hale's hand.)" },
			{ heading = "Sept. 16" },
			"Radio unusable. Not dead: the static has a rhythm to it. Four beats and a rest. Four beats and a rest. I timed it against my watch for an hour. It never drifted, not once, not by a second.",
			"I have been humming something since this morning. I only noticed because I stopped, and the quiet that came after was worse. I don't know the tune. I would remember a tune like that.",
			{ heading = "Sept. 18" },
			"Called it in from the ranger station in town. The man on the other end of the line asked me three times whether I had touched it. I said no. It's the truth.",
			"I didn't need to touch it. When I stood at the rim this evening, it got brighter. When I walked away, it dimmed. When I came back to check whether I'd imagined it, it was brighter than before, as if it had been waiting, and was pleased I'd returned.",
			"Men in grey cars are arriving tomorrow. I'm told I'll be thanked for my work and asked to sign something. I will sign it. I have a daughter.",
			"I'm writing the tune down here so that someone else knows it exists. Four notes rising, one falling. Then the rest.",
		},
	},
